JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Tools Interface) is a native interface that allows you to instrument and manipulate the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) from native code. Class transformations are a type of JVMTI operation that allow you to modify the behavior of Java classes at runtime.

Here are some examples of JVMTI class transformations in Android:

**Example 1: Logging method calls**
```c
#include <jvmti.h>
#include <jni.h>

// ...
jvmtiEnv* jvmti_env;
jclass MyClass;
jmethodID MyApp_ctor;

// Initialize JVMTI and load the MyClass class
jvmti_env = VMInit();
jclass MyClass = (*jvmti_env)->FindClass(jvmti_env, "com/example/MyClass");
jmethodID MyApp_ctor = (*jvmti_env)->GetMethodID(jvmti_env, MyClass, "<init>", "([Ljava/lang/String;)V");

// Transform the class to log method calls
jvmti_env->SetMethodEntryPoints(jvmti_env, MyClass, MyApp_ctor, (jvmti_method_entry_t)log_method_call);
jvmti_env->SetClassTransformer(jvmti_env, MyClass, MyClass, (jvmti_transform_t)transform_class);

// ...

void log_method_call(jvmti_env* env, JNIEnv* js, jclass cls, jmethodID mid) {
    printf("Method call: %s/%s\n", js->GetClassName(cls), js->GetMethodSignature(mid));
}

int transform_class(jvmti_env* env, jclass cls) {
    // Perform any additional class transformation logic here
    return JVMTI_ERROR_NONE;
}
```
This example shows how to use JVMTI to log method calls in a Java class. The `SetMethodEntryPoints` and `SetClassTransformer` operations are used to transform the class and register a method entry point to log method calls.

**Example 2: Intercepting field access**
