Delphi 7 is an older version of the Delphi programming language, but I'll still try to help you with the code to rotate a PDF file.

To rotate a PDF file in Delphi 7, you'll need to use a third-party library that provides PDF manipulation capabilities. One popular option is the `pdflib` library, which is available for download from CodeCentral.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to rotate a PDF file using `pdflib`:
```pascal
uses
  pdflib, pdflpdf;

procedure RotatePDF(PDFFile: String; Rotation: Integer);
var
  PDF: TPdfDocument;
  Page: TPdfPage;
begin
  // Initialize the PDFlib library
  PDFLibInitialize;

  // Open the PDF file
  PDF := TPdfDocument.Create;
  PDF.LoadFromFile(PDFFile);

  // Loop through all pages in the PDF document
  for Page in PDF.Pages do
  begin
    // Rotate the page according to the Rotation parameter
    case Rotation of
      90: Page.Rotate(Page.Width, Page.Height, 90);
      180: Page.Rotate(Page.Width, Page.Height, 180);
      270: Page.Rotate(Page.Width, Page.Height, 270);
    end;
  end;

  // Save the modified PDF document
  PDF.SaveToFile(PDFFile);

  // Clean up
  PDF.Free;
  PDFLibUninitialize;
end;

procedure TForm1.Button1Click(Sender: TObject);
begin
  RotatePDF('input.pdf', 90); // Rotate the PDF 90 degrees clockwise
end;
```
In this example, the `RotatePDF` procedure takes two parameters: the name of the PDF file to rotate and the rotation angle (in degrees). The procedure loads the PDF file using `TPdfDocument`, loops through all pages, rotates each page using the `Rotate` method, and then saves the modified PDF document back to the original file.

Note that the `Rotate` method takes three parameters: the width and height of the page, and the rotation angle in degrees. The rotation is applied around the top-left corner of the page.

